  "id": "Compound::DB00180 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Flunisolide is linked to Nr3c1 through a series of molecular and pharmacological pathways that involve glucocorticoid receptor activation. The evidence suggests that Flunisolide, through its resemblance to Fluocortolone and Desonide, ultimately binds to Nr3c1, a gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor. This binding is supported by the presence of a lowest common ancestor (LCA) between Flunisolide and Desonide, which shares a common pathway to Therapeutic Glucocorticoid and 3-Oxo-Delta(1),Delta(4)-Steroid. The LCA between Flunisolide and Fluticasone Furoate, and between Flunisolide and Beclomethasone, further reinforces the glucocorticoid receptor pathway, as these compounds are known to activate Nr3c1 in various contexts.\n\nThe mechanism of action is further supported by the observed effects of Flunisolide on Candida Albicans Infection and Nasal Septum Perforation, which are mediated by the binding of Flunisolide to Nr3c1. These effects are consistent with the known role of glucocorticoids in modulating immune responses and tissue homeostasis. The integration of these pathways suggests that Flunisolide exerts its effects through a well-established glucocorticoid receptor signaling cascade, which is a core mechanism in the regulation of inflammation and cellular stress responses. The evidence from the dataset supports a direct interaction between Flunisolide and Nr3c1, mediated through the glucocorticoid receptor, and highlights the importance of this receptor in the pathophysiology of the conditions associated with Flunisolide use.",
